1
00:00:00,353 --> 00:00:03,322
That's where this harness comes into play

2
00:00:03,322 --> 00:00:04,702
again. That harness

3
00:00:05,248 --> 00:00:05,697
sends

4
00:00:06,275 --> 00:00:07,912
your input to the model.

5
00:00:09,180 --> 00:00:11,747
So the prompt you entered in Claude Cowork,

6
00:00:11,763 --> 00:00:14,524
for example, the Claude Cowork harness sends

7
00:00:14,636 --> 00:00:16,257
that to the model, but

8
00:00:16,947 --> 00:00:17,974
not just that.

9
00:00:18,809 --> 00:00:21,746
It also sends some extra information which

10
00:00:21,858 --> 00:00:24,458
you don't see to the model along with your

11
00:00:24,506 --> 00:00:27,459
input. For example, descriptions of the

12
00:00:27,555 --> 00:00:30,443
tools the model may use. Tools like reading

13
00:00:30,507 --> 00:00:32,417
a file, searching the web, running some

14
00:00:32,513 --> 00:00:35,561
code, or more. And in addition, the harness

15
00:00:35,561 --> 00:00:38,995
may also send some extra instructions, for

16
00:00:39,011 --> 00:00:41,369
example, regarding how the model should

17
00:00:41,497 --> 00:00:44,161
behave or how it should in general tackle

18
00:00:44,161 --> 00:00:46,840
problems and so on. But that is something

19
00:00:46,952 --> 00:00:49,343
we'll get back to later. That information is

20
00:00:49,375 --> 00:00:52,487
sent to the model by the harness. The model

21
00:00:52,567 --> 00:00:54,955
then, as mentioned, can't execute those

22
00:00:54,987 --> 00:00:58,192
tools, but it can decide based on that

23
00:00:58,192 --> 00:01:00,276
information it gets, based on the prompt,

24
00:01:00,356 --> 00:01:02,007
based on the instructions, and based on

25
00:01:02,039 --> 00:01:03,289
these tool descriptions,

26
00:01:03,946 --> 00:01:05,741
what it would like to do next.

27
00:01:06,366 --> 00:01:09,059
Because a model, since it can output text,

28
00:01:09,251 --> 00:01:12,040
can of course replicate a thought process.

29
00:01:12,456 --> 00:01:15,005
You could also write down a thought process

30
00:01:15,085 --> 00:01:17,601
on a piece of paper, right? A model can do

31
00:01:17,601 --> 00:01:20,229
the same. It can describe a thought process

32
00:01:20,229 --> 00:01:21,801
to reason through a problem. Problem.

33
00:01:22,556 --> 00:01:25,366
And by doing that, since it has that extra

34
00:01:25,366 --> 00:01:27,743
information about tools it may use,

35
00:01:28,337 --> 00:01:30,521
it can reason about these tools too.

36
00:01:31,212 --> 00:01:33,797
And it can come to the conclusion based on a

37
00:01:33,797 --> 00:01:35,692
thought process, which could look like this,

38
00:01:36,431 --> 00:01:39,772
that it wants to use the read file tool for

39
00:01:40,141 --> 00:01:42,036
the problem you threw at it.

40
00:01:42,807 --> 00:01:44,847
So that is simply the result of a thought

41
00:01:44,879 --> 00:01:47,047
process. Now, as mentioned, the final output

42
00:01:47,063 --> 00:01:48,155
of a large language model

43
00:01:49,534 --> 00:01:52,644
But that text can, of course, also simply be

44
00:01:52,884 --> 00:01:54,808
the name of the tool it wants to use, for

45
00:01:54,808 --> 00:01:57,052
example. And that's indeed how it works. The

46
00:01:57,052 --> 00:01:57,677
harness

47
00:01:58,286 --> 00:02:00,434
sends your prompt and those tool

48
00:02:00,434 --> 00:02:02,598
descriptions to the model. The model thinks

49
00:02:02,598 --> 00:02:05,644
about it and then sends back a tool call

50
00:02:05,644 --> 00:02:06,173
request,

51
00:02:06,846 --> 00:02:09,507
which in reality doesn't look like that. It

52
00:02:09,636 --> 00:02:11,832
has a more machine-readable format, but that

53
00:02:11,832 --> 00:02:12,906
is how you can think of it.

54
00:02:13,579 --> 00:02:14,669
The harness then reads that request.

55
00:02:14,926 --> 00:02:16,625
Receives that response,

56
00:02:17,250 --> 00:02:20,408
analyzes it, and then executes that tool

57
00:02:20,472 --> 00:02:21,097
call request.

58
00:02:21,931 --> 00:02:23,886
So the harness, the software running on your

59
00:02:23,918 --> 00:02:26,179
system, potentially something like Claude

60
00:02:26,179 --> 00:02:29,481
Cowork, that's the thing that actually picks

61
00:02:29,673 --> 00:02:32,623
up that tool call request and executes it.

62
00:02:33,056 --> 00:02:36,214
If in Claude Cowork you see a log like this

63
00:02:36,743 --> 00:02:38,233
reading files on your computer,

64
00:02:38,826 --> 00:02:41,327
that was the harness, that was Claude Cowork

65
00:02:41,552 --> 00:02:44,090
reading the files, but it did that because

66
00:02:44,122 --> 00:02:46,500
the model requested it. So the harness does

67
00:02:46,532 --> 00:02:49,584
that. And the result of a tool call, so the

68
00:02:49,584 --> 00:02:52,363
content of a file, for example, is then sent

69
00:02:52,476 --> 00:02:54,532
back to the model. And therefore the model

70
00:02:54,580 --> 00:02:57,327
now knows a bit more. Initially, it had your

71
00:02:57,456 --> 00:02:59,753
prompt and it came to the conclusion that it

72
00:02:59,785 --> 00:03:02,436
wants to read a file. It requested that. The

73
00:03:02,436 --> 00:03:05,697
harness then executed that tool, sent the

74
00:03:05,729 --> 00:03:08,283
results back to the model. So in the next

75
00:03:08,283 --> 00:03:10,674
round, where the model gets called again,

76
00:03:11,251 --> 00:03:13,512
it still knows about your prompt and the

77
00:03:13,512 --> 00:03:15,966
available tools and the extra instructions,

78
00:03:16,463 --> 00:03:19,013
either because the model provider stores

79
00:03:19,125 --> 00:03:20,264
that on their servers

80
00:03:20,873 --> 00:03:20,922
or

81
00:03:21,595 --> 00:03:24,306
also very common because the harness stores

82
00:03:24,418 --> 00:03:26,583
this entire conversation with all the tool

83
00:03:26,583 --> 00:03:29,470
calls and so on internally and sends the

84
00:03:29,470 --> 00:03:31,795
entire conversation to the model with every

85
00:03:31,795 --> 00:03:34,858
turn. But either way, the model knows about

86
00:03:34,858 --> 00:03:37,023
the entire conversation with all the

87
00:03:37,071 --> 00:03:39,782
details, all the context. Including the

88
00:03:39,815 --> 00:03:42,029
result of that tool call it requested.

89
00:03:42,687 --> 00:03:45,047
So it has more information in round 2

90
00:03:45,592 --> 00:03:46,732
than it did in round 1.

91
00:03:47,277 --> 00:03:48,754
And that's how it can work through a

92
00:03:48,882 --> 00:03:51,402
problem. It can request more tool calls and

93
00:03:51,434 --> 00:03:53,537
again, send that back to the harness, the

94
00:03:53,537 --> 00:03:57,035
harness executes it and so on until it has

95
00:03:57,148 --> 00:03:59,699
gathered enough information across multiple

96
00:03:59,699 --> 00:04:03,214
rounds that it's done. So the model does not

97
00:04:03,391 --> 00:04:06,999
make a tool call. It instead requests it and

98
00:04:06,999 --> 00:04:09,420
the harness executes it. The model just

99
00:04:09,420 --> 00:04:11,825
decides. And therefore, after each round,

100
00:04:12,145 --> 00:04:15,047
the model may either continue and request

101
00:04:15,047 --> 00:04:15,945
another tool call

102
00:04:16,618 --> 00:04:18,798
or it may be done. It did everything it

103
00:04:18,798 --> 00:04:21,331
needs to do for the task and it outputs one

104
00:04:21,668 --> 00:04:23,912
final message at the end. That's always what

105
00:04:23,912 --> 00:04:25,692
you get in the end. But along the way, it

106
00:04:25,756 --> 00:04:28,305
may, of course, through tool calls, have

107
00:04:28,417 --> 00:04:31,351
changed files, executed code, all kinds of

108
00:04:31,447 --> 00:04:33,788
other things. That's the idea behind such an

109
00:04:33,788 --> 00:04:36,370
agent. So you get a final report. But before

110
00:04:36,370 --> 00:04:38,491
that, the work already happened in those

111
00:04:38,604 --> 00:04:41,351
laps, in those rounds. And that is simply

112
00:04:41,576 --> 00:04:45,368
the agent loop that keeps on going until a

113
00:04:45,433 --> 00:04:46,525
task is completed.

114
00:04:47,200 --> 00:04:49,691
And tools like Claude Cowork, which are the

115
00:04:49,691 --> 00:04:53,161
harness, simply run such an agent loop

116
00:04:53,161 --> 00:04:53,836
internally,

117
00:04:54,495 --> 00:04:56,455
provide all those tool definitions,

118
00:04:56,503 --> 00:04:59,106
instructions, and of course also your prompt

119
00:04:59,171 --> 00:04:59,396
to

120
00:05:00,119 --> 00:05:02,933
the model, to that loop. And then keep the

121
00:05:02,933 --> 00:05:05,171
loop going by executing those tool call

122
00:05:05,203 --> 00:05:07,827
requests and feeding the results of those

123
00:05:07,859 --> 00:05:11,337
tool calls back into the model until a task

124
00:05:11,659 --> 00:05:13,816
is done. That is the entire idea.
